#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087443 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "bea23649-e835-540a-b238-7b89ed330cd3" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Inverse of a 2x2 Matrix"}] :question/id #uuid "318fc39e-0efe-5b98-968c-ff4408706471" :question/math-academy-id "e-4502" :question/problem "Calculate $A^{-1},$ where\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Given a $2 \\times 2$ matrix\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}a & b \\\\ c & d\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nthe inverse of $A$ can be found using the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{det(A)}[\\begin{aligned}d & -b \\\\ -c & a\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute the determinant:\n\n$$\n\\det(A) = 1 \\cdot 1 - 0 \\cdot (-2) = 1\n$$\n\nUsing the formula, then, the inverse is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{det(A)}[\\begin{aligned}d & -b \\\\ -c & a\\end{aligned}]. \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 & -0 \\\\ -(-2) & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can verify the result as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A{A}^{-1} & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={I}_{2}\\,✓ \\\\ {A}^{-1}A & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={I}_{2}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087444 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4c6056ee-15f3-5b5c-8a14-173ea1da1381" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Singular Matrices"}] :question/id #uuid "195d7dab-edf2-536b-a7b7-d1c16c7b1071" :question/math-academy-id "e-4503" :question/problem "Which of the matrices below are singular?\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :question/worked-solution "- For the matrix $A$, we have\n\n$$\n\\det({A})=1 \\cdot 1 - 1 \\cdot 1 = 0.\n$$\n\nBecause $\\det(A)=0,$ we conclude that $A$ is not invertible. Since $A$ is a square matrix that is not invertible, we also say that $A$ is singular.\n\n- The matrix $B$ is a $3 \\times 2$ matrix. So, it is not invertible. Since the matrix is not square, we can't say that it's singular.\n\n- For the matrix $C$, we have\n\n$$\n\\det({C})=3 \\cdot 3 - (-3) \\cdot 3 = 18.\n$$\n\nBecause $\\det(C) \\neq 0,$ we conclude that $C$ is invertible.\n\nTherefore, only $A$ is singular."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087445 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4e64e0c3-f8f5-5c38-ae75-1aed92e0ac01" :knowledge-point/title "Solving for a Variable in a Singular Matrix"}] :question/id #uuid "ef06dd89-501b-5d2b-a56d-4554a049a91d" :question/math-academy-id "e-4504" :question/problem "If \n$[\\begin{aligned}1-k & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1-k\\end{aligned}]$\n is a singular matrix, calculate the value(s) of $k.$" :question/worked-solution "The matrix $A$ is singular if and only if $\\det(A)=0.$ Calculating the determinant, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & =(1-k)(1-k)-4 \\\\  & ={k}^{2}-2k-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we set the determinant equal to $0$ and solve for $k\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{k}^{2}-2k-3 & =0 \\\\ (k-3)(k+1) & =0 \\\\ k & =3,-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the values of $k$ are $k=3$ and $k=-1.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087928 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4c6056ee-15f3-5b5c-8a14-173ea1da1381" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Singular Matrices"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a48fa181-4b87-58a5-8982-f7bd1543ffbb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ and $C$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186084580} {:answer/id #uuid "b2bfd71d-be6a-5213-b584-494fc8d57128" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$C$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186084581} {:answer/id #uuid "dff8ce25-a4b5-5ebd-8746-1198b79cbdc6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ and $B$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186084582} {:answer/id #uuid "fb605f8d-af27-5ebe-a9dc-17cebc5bec9b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$B$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186084583} {:answer/id #uuid "359451d1-a627-5152-b123-4515b7d7b5af" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$B$ and $C$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186084584}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b2bfd71d-be6a-5213-b584-494fc8d57128" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$C$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186084581} :answer-field/id #uuid "f021e344-b1e5-51fb-adc9-8fe7c497cc1c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186084579}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "dd3fba16-5fcc-50bc-8866-44ee67a3b9b6" :question/math-academy-id "q-11718" :question/problem "Which of the matrices below are singular?\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,B=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}],\\,C=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/worked-solution "- For the matrix $A$, we have\n\n$$\ndet(A)=0\\cdot 0-1\\cdot 1=-1.\n$$\n\nBecause $det(A)\\ne 0,$ we conclude that $A$ is invertible.\n\n- The matrix $B$ is a $3\\times 1$ matrix. So, it is not invertible. Since the matrix is not square, we can't say that it's singular.\n\n- For the matrix $C$, we have\n\n$$\ndet(C)=1\\cdot 0-0\\cdot 1=0.\n$$\n\nBecause $det(C)=0,$ we conclude that $C$ is not invertible. Since $C$ is a square matrix that is not invertible, we also say that $C$ is singular.\n\nTherefore, only $C$ is singular."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087932 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4c6056ee-15f3-5b5c-8a14-173ea1da1381" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Singular Matrices"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d194789e-6aed-5c66-b8e7-57991be5788f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186084604} {:answer/id #uuid "e6a2713a-4f88-52f3-92c2-676deeb127eb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$, $B$, and $C$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186084605} {:answer/id #uuid "34dcc9de-a143-528f-9a0e-24d787fb1659" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$B$ and $C$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186084606} {:answer/id #uuid "6b9d41d6-83a1-59d9-adab-76f01f80e082" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ and $B$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186084607} {:answer/id #uuid "7abfd5c4-c04b-591b-9ebf-44f6feb9585a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$C$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186084608}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6b9d41d6-83a1-59d9-adab-76f01f80e082" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ and $B$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186084607} :answer-field/id #uuid "5d1dd334-a4ec-50b2-8944-ae9ba995bb56" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186084603}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d6fdbc09-a579-52c0-b98c-5f14a5229abc" :question/math-academy-id "q-11725" :question/problem "Which of the matrices below does **not** have an inverse?\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 5 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1 & -1\\end{aligned}],\\,B=[\\begin{aligned}6 & 8 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{aligned}],\\,C=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 \\\\ 5 & 5\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/worked-solution "- The matrix $A$ is a $2\\times 3$ matrix. So, it is not invertible.\n\n- For the matrix $B$, we have\n\n$$\ndet(B)=6\\cdot 4-8\\cdot 3=0.\n$$\n\nBecause $det(B)=0,$ we conclude that $B$ is not invertible.\n\n- For the matrix $C$, we have\n\n$$\ndet(C)=(-1)\\cdot 5-1\\cdot 5=-10.\n$$\n\nBecause $det(C)\\ne 0,$ we conclude that $C$ is invertible.\n\nTherefore, only $A$ and $B$ do not have an inverse."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186277068 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "bea23649-e835-540a-b238-7b89ed330cd3" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Inverse of a 2x2 Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9b0e4e4f-019b-54ff-a8b6-17c2dfffcb34" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ -1 & -2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277070} {:answer/id #uuid "8867e674-61b8-52d5-8b4f-392b6107371a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277071} {:answer/id #uuid "3a853d67-92d5-535e-b468-8e02dad2a667" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277072} {:answer/id #uuid "7c794efe-e4d2-5478-8cef-b8f0335411da" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277073} {:answer/id #uuid "8dd9b462-c5f5-514f-bd5e-cd2427a802cf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277074}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7c794efe-e4d2-5478-8cef-b8f0335411da" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277073} :answer-field/id #uuid "11f04062-ea30-5d75-a3e3-2839fb18deea" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277069}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "469cce88-f2bf-5c3e-aa47-9c15a4251782" :question/math-academy-id "q-127742" :question/problem "Calculate ${B}^{-1}$, where \n$B=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}].$" :question/worked-solution "Given a $2\\times 2$ matrix\n\n$$\nB=[\\begin{aligned}a & b \\\\ c & d\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nthe inverse of $B$ can be found using the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{B}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{det(B)}[\\begin{aligned}d & -b \\\\ -c & a\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute the determinant:\n\n$$\ndet(B)=2\\cdot 0-1\\cdot 1=-1\n$$\n\nUsing the formula, then, the inverse is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{B}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{det(B)}[\\begin{aligned}d & -b \\\\ -c & a\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(-1)}[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can verify the result as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}B{B}^{-1} & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={I}_{2}\\,✓ \\\\ {B}^{-1}B & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={I}_{2}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186303503 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4c6056ee-15f3-5b5c-8a14-173ea1da1381" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Singular Matrices"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "db222ce2-d658-59e8-abed-720804bd2f85" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ and $C$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303505} {:answer/id #uuid "9ffc3da3-6ece-5f07-aa43-96a827f4751d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303506} {:answer/id #uuid "417850f7-e746-50ba-baca-15037850a3d8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ and $B$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303507} {:answer/id #uuid "fa4a2fb7-63c1-5e6f-9be6-7787e63f53af" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$C$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303508} {:answer/id #uuid "4ebdb491-448d-58e9-9879-1b62216ed7af" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$B$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303509}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9ffc3da3-6ece-5f07-aa43-96a827f4751d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303506} :answer-field/id #uuid "66c0f296-846d-563e-b7ef-ec9210792f71" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303504}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "8bc7e218-c432-5c7d-93cb-660cd4ed939b" :question/math-academy-id "q-127751" :question/problem "Which of the matrices below are singular?\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,B=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,C=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 3 \\\\ -3 & 5\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/worked-solution "- For the matrix $A$, we have\n\n$$\ndet(A)=(-1)\\cdot 0-1\\cdot 0=0.\n$$\n\nBecause $det(A)=0,$ we conclude that $A$ is not invertible. Since $A$ is a square matrix that is not invertible, we also say that $A$ is singular.\n\n- The matrix $B$ is a $2\\times 1$ matrix. So, it is not invertible. Since the matrix is not square, we can't say that it's singular.\n\n- For the matrix $C$, we have\n\n$$\ndet(C)=(-2)\\cdot 5-(-3)\\cdot (3)=-1.\n$$\n\nBecause $det(C)\\ne 0,$ we conclude that $C$ is invertible.\n\nTherefore, only $A$ is singular."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186303510 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "bea23649-e835-540a-b238-7b89ed330cd3" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Inverse of a 2x2 Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "aeb2f2a6-b6e4-51c5-bd57-9a38c7fd1c53" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303512} {:answer/id #uuid "6b2d1a12-5ca3-57f2-aecb-39170cfe9238" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303513}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "aeb2f2a6-b6e4-51c5-bd57-9a38c7fd1c53" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303512} :answer-field/id #uuid "c103387a-b1a2-5b9d-b920-00f020bc9fa4" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303511} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1f23df9e-6a87-594b-8fb1-effbc79fd4ae" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303515}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1f23df9e-6a87-594b-8fb1-effbc79fd4ae" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303515} :answer-field/id #uuid "0f4bc053-cbd1-5f68-af31-eead6a628030" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303514} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9dd88486-a7bc-5701-b4f0-0d0979c46bdb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303517}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9dd88486-a7bc-5701-b4f0-0d0979c46bdb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303517} :answer-field/id #uuid "979fc8f9-5964-5ce8-bb9c-f1c2c6b7edfe" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303516}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ca4de43e-fb29-5656-88a3-862277669b5e" :question/math-academy-id "q-316753" :question/problem "Calculate ${A}^{-1},$ where\n$A=[\\begin{aligned}-6 & 3 \\\\ -5 & 2\\end{aligned}].$\n\n|  |  | {{field-1}} | {{field-2}} |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${A}^{-1}=\\,\\,$ |  |  |  |  |\n|  |  | {{field-3}} | $-2$ |  |" :question/worked-solution "Given a $2\\times 2$ matrix\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}a & b \\\\ c & d\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nthe inverse of $A$ can be found using the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{det(A)}[\\begin{aligned}d & -b \\\\ -c & a\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute the determinant:\n\n$$\ndet(A)=(-6)\\cdot 2-3\\cdot (-5)=3\n$$\n\nUsing the formula, then, the inverse is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{det(A)}[\\begin{aligned}d & -b \\\\ -c & a\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}[\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 \\\\ -(-5) & -6\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}[\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 \\\\ 5 & -6\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2/3 & -1 \\\\ 5/3 & -2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can verify the result as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A{A}^{-1} & =[\\begin{aligned}-6 & 3 \\\\ -5 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2/3 & -1 \\\\ 5/3 & -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={I}_{2}\\,✓ \\\\ {A}^{-1}A & =[\\begin{aligned}2/3 & -1 \\\\ 5/3 & -2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-6 & 3 \\\\ -5 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={I}_{2}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186303518 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "bea23649-e835-540a-b238-7b89ed330cd3" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Inverse of a 2x2 Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1358471d-c759-5287-a536-bdd080a221d4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303520}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1358471d-c759-5287-a536-bdd080a221d4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303520} :answer-field/id #uuid "4fb11755-d938-5d97-835a-f02b7c3d9428" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303519} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a0c2388a-d6d7-5d5d-a4af-e901c5acbe23" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303522}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a0c2388a-d6d7-5d5d-a4af-e901c5acbe23" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303522} :answer-field/id #uuid "1e6a21f5-5a60-5523-a38a-6b9a134383c3" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303521} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9cfd6101-e3d1-5b4e-9788-ecbf3618d170" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303524}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9cfd6101-e3d1-5b4e-9788-ecbf3618d170" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303524} :answer-field/id #uuid "692ea9bf-704e-5dc4-b205-f3525dab8498" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303523}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "6835b4df-301d-5b0d-b2b8-2bb025d353e1" :question/math-academy-id "q-316768" :question/problem "Calculate ${A}^{-1},$ where\n$A=[\\begin{aligned}5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}].$\n\n|  |  | {{field-1}} | $0$ |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${A}^{-1}=\\,\\,$ |  |  |  |  |\n|  |  | {{field-2}} | {{field-3}} |  |" :question/worked-solution "Given a $2\\times 2$ matrix\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}a & b \\\\ c & d\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nthe inverse of $A$ can be found using the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{det(A)}[\\begin{aligned}d & -b \\\\ -c & a\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute the determinant:\n\n$$\ndet(A)=5\\cdot (-2)-0\\cdot 0=-10\n$$\n\nUsing the formula, then, the inverse is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{det(A)}[\\begin{aligned}d & -b \\\\ -c & a\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{-10}[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 5\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1/5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1/2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can verify the result as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A{A}^{-1} & =[\\begin{aligned}5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1/5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1/2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={I}_{2}\\,✓ \\\\ {A}^{-1}A & =[\\begin{aligned}1/5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1/2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={I}_{2}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186293818 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4e64e0c3-f8f5-5c38-ae75-1aed92e0ac01" :knowledge-point/title "Solving for a Variable in a Singular Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b143ed6d-3f8a-5a5f-9b22-6a3acb7a18ab" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293820} {:answer/id #uuid "b0d1eee9-040f-510d-b5a6-b707a05db1ec" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293821}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b0d1eee9-040f-510d-b5a6-b707a05db1ec" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293821} :answer-field/id #uuid "88f236f3-feef-563d-a98b-ee60e0ff782a" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293819}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "0047a425-3434-51cf-8bea-b68dedd67cd7" :question/math-academy-id "q-316782" :question/problem "If $B=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 3x+2 & 2x-4\\end{aligned}]$\nis a singular matrix, calculate the value of $x.$\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The matrix $B$ is singular if and only if $det(B)=0.$ Calculating the determinant, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(B) & =1\\cdot (2x-4)-2\\cdot (3x+2) \\\\  & =2x-4-6x-4 \\\\  & =-4x-8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we set the determinant equal to $0$ and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-4x-8 & =0 \\\\ -4x & =8 \\\\ x & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186303525 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4e64e0c3-f8f5-5c38-ae75-1aed92e0ac01" :knowledge-point/title "Solving for a Variable in a Singular Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9cb3c9e2-3c64-577f-aeb1-5d5e914b8b41" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303527}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9cb3c9e2-3c64-577f-aeb1-5d5e914b8b41" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303527} :answer-field/id #uuid "ffa74d1b-4335-56bb-aab7-44ba0eb0ffe8" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303526}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "5443d517-7df6-5344-80b9-17b5b74831e0" :question/math-academy-id "q-316783" :question/problem "If $B=[\\begin{aligned}5x & -1 \\\\ 6x-8 & 2\\end{aligned}]$\nis a singular matrix, calculate the value of $x.$\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The matrix $B$ is singular if and only if $det(B)=0.$ Calculating the determinant, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(B) & =(5x)\\cdot 2-(-1)\\cdot (6x-8) \\\\  & =10x+6x-8 \\\\  & =16x-8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we set the determinant equal to $0$ and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}16x-8 & =0 \\\\ 16x & =8 \\\\ x & =\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186303528 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4e64e0c3-f8f5-5c38-ae75-1aed92e0ac01" :knowledge-point/title "Solving for a Variable in a Singular Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e3707f8a-5ad7-5365-8eae-901a4d71871c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "26" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303530} {:answer/id #uuid "20dea9c5-a46a-5cf9-a2e4-7aa6a62e9727" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-26" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303531}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "20dea9c5-a46a-5cf9-a2e4-7aa6a62e9727" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-26" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303531} :answer-field/id #uuid "f63b0cec-8348-5835-a6df-4b735dd0bcc4" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303529}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "66c05155-e66f-5d77-9b4e-130629dd787b" :question/math-academy-id "q-316784" :question/problem "If $B=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & -x+1 \\\\ 5 & 2x+7\\end{aligned}]$\nis a singular matrix, calculate the value of $x.$\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The matrix $B$ is singular if and only if $det(B)=0.$ Calculating the determinant, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(B) & =(-3)\\cdot (2x+7)-5\\cdot (-x+1) \\\\  & =-6x-21+5x-5 \\\\  & =-x-26.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we set the determinant equal to $0$ and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-x-26 & =0 \\\\ -x & =26 \\\\ x & =-26\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186303532 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4e64e0c3-f8f5-5c38-ae75-1aed92e0ac01" :knowledge-point/title "Solving for a Variable in a Singular Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b75012e3-e492-5792-9719-1d397d71cf66" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303534}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b75012e3-e492-5792-9719-1d397d71cf66" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303534} :answer-field/id #uuid "e3c73af9-51b6-5091-b134-07564ff2febe" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303533} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1520c08b-c044-52a0-bba9-fde2eed71cec" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303536}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1520c08b-c044-52a0-bba9-fde2eed71cec" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303536} :answer-field/id #uuid "79908c2f-632b-513d-aa7a-fed9ae5536d8" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303535}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "8b310642-ede6-5a4f-a56c-989f0c39c332" :question/math-academy-id "q-316828" :question/problem "Let $B=[\\begin{aligned}2x+1 & x-40 \\\\ 1 & -x\\end{aligned}]$\nbe a singular matrix. **In ascending order**, the values of $x$ are\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}$\\,$ and $\\,x=$\n{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "The matrix $B$ is singular if and only if $det(B)=0.$ Calculating the determinant, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(B) & =(2x+1)\\cdot (-x)-(x-40)\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =-2{x}^{2}-x-x+40 \\\\  & =-2{x}^{2}-2x+40.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we set the determinant equal to $0$ and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2{x}^{2}-2x+40 & =0 \\\\ -2({x}^{2}+x-20) & =0 \\\\ -2(x-4)(x+5) & =0 \\\\ x & =4,-5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, in ascending order, the values of $x$ are\n\n$$\nx=-5\\,\\text{and}\\,x=4.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186303537 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4c6056ee-15f3-5b5c-8a14-173ea1da1381" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Singular Matrices"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4a83cf5c-2492-5e7d-bd1f-81cb45d8045e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$U$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303539} {:answer/id #uuid "479ede7d-ca69-5627-b741-68a5088f3990" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$T$ and $V$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303540} {:answer/id #uuid "293a000b-5fa3-57f5-9c73-c304496bfc14" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$T$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303541} {:answer/id #uuid "5f05dd50-60ac-56b8-8d96-1693f5941558" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$V$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303542} {:answer/id #uuid "bc8c0e80-c30c-50b3-82ad-9bbd5045fc90" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$U$ and $V$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303543}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5f05dd50-60ac-56b8-8d96-1693f5941558" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$V$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303542} :answer-field/id #uuid "75efd0d7-75e1-5283-b402-be9d95bd287d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303538}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ff070848-777f-5635-9918-6da54f4ec8b2" :question/math-academy-id "q-71937" :question/problem "Which of the matrices below are singular?\n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}5 & -8 & 7 & -6 \\\\ 1 & -2 & 3 & -4\\end{aligned}],\\,U=[\\begin{aligned}3 & -15 \\\\ -1 & 4\\end{aligned}],\\,V=[\\begin{aligned}6 & 3 \\\\ 10 & 5\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/worked-solution "- The matrix $T$ is a $2\\times 4$ matrix. So, it is not invertible. Since the matrix is not square, we can't say that it's singular.\n\n- For the matrix $U$, we have\n\n$$\ndet(U)=3\\cdot 4-(-1)\\cdot (-15)=-3.\n$$\n\nBecause $det(U)\\ne 0,$ we conclude that $U$ is invertible.\n\n- For the matrix $V$, we have\n\n$$\ndet(V)=6\\cdot 5-10\\cdot 3=0.\n$$\n\nBecause $det(V)=0,$ we conclude that $V$ is not invertible. Since $V$ is a square matrix that is not invertible, we also say that $V$ is singular.\n\nTherefore, only $V$ is singular."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186277113 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4c6056ee-15f3-5b5c-8a14-173ea1da1381" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Singular Matrices"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "08eb2ea7-f792-5e17-ba62-6cb93b9a1555" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$X$ and $Z$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277115} {:answer/id #uuid "6b708952-cde1-539c-b2ec-210459186665" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$X$ and $Y$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277116} {:answer/id #uuid "3e2c51de-d9e4-53e2-8df8-7c5a5dda12ce" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$Y$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277117} {:answer/id #uuid "d7884f22-d342-599e-b966-702453ae8925" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$Z$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277118} {:answer/id #uuid "c5ed6083-9a87-5d3f-8c78-32388cdb4327" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$X$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277119}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c5ed6083-9a87-5d3f-8c78-32388cdb4327" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$X$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277119} :answer-field/id #uuid "bd54c950-30a6-5bde-8785-a5144115b030" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277114}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f34f6a84-b6d1-59f1-84c8-b1742c3a5c7a" :question/math-academy-id "q-71938" :question/problem "Which of the matrices below does **not** have an inverse?\n\n$$\nX=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ -4 & 6 \\\\ 10 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,Y=[\\begin{aligned}6 & 5 \\\\ 7 & 6\\end{aligned}],\\,Z=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 \\\\ 10 & 5\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/worked-solution "- The matrix $X$ is a $3\\times 2$ matrix. So, it is not invertible.\n\n- For the matrix $Y$, we have\n\n$$\ndet(Y)=6\\cdot 6-7\\cdot 5=1.\n$$\n\nBecause $det(Y)\\ne 0,$ we conclude that $Y$ is invertible.\n\n- For the matrix $Z$, we have\n\n$$\ndet(Z)=(-2)\\cdot 5-10\\cdot 1=-20.\n$$\n\nBecause $det(Z)\\ne 0,$ we conclude that $Z$ is invertible.\n\nTherefore, only $X$ does not have an inverse."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186303544 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4e64e0c3-f8f5-5c38-ae75-1aed92e0ac01" :knowledge-point/title "Solving for a Variable in a Singular Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fa2f4b1a-4076-5d23-92af-36d061da630b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "c=\\pm 5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303546} {:answer/id #uuid "5189922d-eb94-5d49-8fc8-c514cadda484" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "c=5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303547} {:answer/id #uuid "42467990-ad8c-56f7-89f2-e087fe3bf2c9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "c=-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303548} {:answer/id #uuid "06b8493a-99ff-5e6c-9a77-45c69605c0c6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "c=6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303549} {:answer/id #uuid "abdcb851-7d2c-5f10-a66e-50ee99c035c7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "c=\\pm 6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303550}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fa2f4b1a-4076-5d23-92af-36d061da630b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "c=\\pm 5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303546} :answer-field/id #uuid "b8638e24-5ed1-52ea-8e76-4fdcef0463c6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303545}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "8c22c847-601a-5a56-9c84-b8ca008f9f6a" :question/math-academy-id "q-71968" :question/problem "If $H=[\\begin{aligned}5 & c \\\\ c & 5\\end{aligned}]$ is a singular matrix, calculate the value(s) of $c.$" :question/worked-solution "The matrix $H$ is singular if and only if $det(H)=0.$ Calculating the determinant, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(H) & =5\\cdot 5-c\\cdot c \\\\  & =25-{c}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we set the determinant equal to $0$ and solve for $c:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}25-{c}^{2} & =0 \\\\ {c}^{2} & =25 \\\\ c & =\\pm 5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the values of $c$ are $c=\\pm 5.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186303551 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "bea23649-e835-540a-b238-7b89ed330cd3" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Inverse of a 2x2 Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ceb8c988-7f92-5c04-b601-54a5e38af944" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}[\\begin{aligned}7 & 3 \\\\ 8 & 4\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303553} {:answer/id #uuid "87321a7e-0fc4-5e5b-a9f6-da27b92468cb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}[\\begin{aligned}-7 & 3 \\\\ 8 & -4\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303554} {:answer/id #uuid "c485af0d-eb54-5bae-ab4e-fbe45131fe3b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}[\\begin{aligned}7 & -3 \\\\ -8 & 4\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303555} {:answer/id #uuid "3459b861-aeb7-55f5-b0c9-27620c047db1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}7 & -3 \\\\ -8 & 4\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303556} {:answer/id #uuid "fe1f879a-709a-589a-97e4-74a95bce36f0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}[\\begin{aligned}-7 & 8 \\\\ 3 & -4\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303557}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c485af0d-eb54-5bae-ab4e-fbe45131fe3b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}[\\begin{aligned}7 & -3 \\\\ -8 & 4\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303555} :answer-field/id #uuid "a3dfd702-1b82-588f-8458-2013f9b01d8f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303552}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "9e0cdcdd-62cd-58bc-822f-b8a4f1afe9a2" :question/math-academy-id "q-7921" :question/problem "Calculate ${E}^{-1}$, where\n$E=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 3 \\\\ 8 & 7\\end{aligned}].$" :question/worked-solution "Given a $2\\times 2$ matrix\n\n$$\nE=[\\begin{aligned}a & b \\\\ c & d\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nthe inverse of $E$ can be found using the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{det(E)}[\\begin{aligned}d & -b \\\\ -c & a\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute the determinant:\n\n$$\ndet(E)=4\\cdot 7-8\\cdot 3=4\n$$\n\nUsing the formula, then, the inverse is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{det(E)}[\\begin{aligned}d & -b \\\\ -c & a\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}[\\begin{aligned}7 & -3 \\\\ -8 & 4\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can verify the result as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}E{E}^{-1} & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & 3 \\\\ 8 & 7\\end{aligned}]\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}[\\begin{aligned}7 & -3 \\\\ -8 & 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={I}_{2}\\,✓ \\\\ {E}^{-1}E & =\\frac{1}{4}[\\begin{aligned}7 & -3 \\\\ -8 & 4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}4 & 3 \\\\ 8 & 7\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={I}_{2}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186303558 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "bea23649-e835-540a-b238-7b89ed330cd3" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Inverse of a 2x2 Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3e5ae171-e8a9-56d9-a99a-92230b32232c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -1 \\\\ -5 & -3\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303560} {:answer/id #uuid "c99fb2ac-cd22-5185-a429-77e6d954c5bd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 5 & 3\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303561} {:answer/id #uuid "389d33f3-75b5-554d-92be-f3684248830a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -1 \\\\ -5 & -3\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303562} {:answer/id #uuid "87726960-08eb-5e22-8d68-4164a69dd4ae" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 5 & 3\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303563} {:answer/id #uuid "60253841-cd5b-56d2-8a5e-e36b387ae31c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -1 \\\\ -5 & -3\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303564}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "389d33f3-75b5-554d-92be-f3684248830a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -1 \\\\ -5 & -3\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303562} :answer-field/id #uuid "dbc603e3-b802-563f-b582-3c52fbdf5764" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303559}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e922303e-f1e5-5b8d-b2da-04cdac58e3ea" :question/math-academy-id "q-7945" :question/problem "Calculate ${Q}^{-1}$, where\n$Q=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 1 \\\\ 5 & -2\\end{aligned}].$" :question/worked-solution "Given a $2\\times 2$ matrix\n\n$$\nQ=[\\begin{aligned}a & b \\\\ c & d\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nthe inverse of $Q$ can be found using the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{det(Q)}[\\begin{aligned}d & -b \\\\ -c & a\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute the determinant:\n\n$$\ndet(Q)=(-3)\\cdot (-2)-5\\cdot 1=1\n$$\n\nUsing the formula, then, the inverse is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{det(Q)}[\\begin{aligned}d & -b \\\\ -c & a\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{1}[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -1 \\\\ -5 & -3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -1 \\\\ -5 & -3\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can verify the result as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q{Q}^{-1} & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 1 \\\\ 5 & -2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-2 & -1 \\\\ -5 & -3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={I}_{2}\\,✓ \\\\ {Q}^{-1}Q & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -1 \\\\ -5 & -3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-3 & 1 \\\\ 5 & -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={I}_{2}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
